'''Cardio-respiratory coherence''' is
a physiological state in which heart-rate, respiration, and blood
pressure synchronise at approximately 0.1 Hz (about six cycles per
minute). It is the trained-state target of the
[[HelmKit_Mk0.5|HelmKit Mk0.5]] L0+L1 biofeedback floor.

== 1. The 0.1 Hz resonance ==

The cardiovascular system has a natural oscillatory mode at ~0.1 Hz
driven by the baroreflex. Breathing at the same rate phase-locks
the respiratory-sinus-arrhythmia HRV oscillation, the baroreflex
oscillation, and the Mayer-wave blood-pressure oscillation; the
combined signal in this state is much larger than any of the three
individually.

The L0 paced-breathing pacer drives the wearer toward 0.1 Hz
respiration (~6 bpm). The L1 closed-loop biofeedback measures
the resulting HRV coherence in real time and renders it back as
an audio harmonic the wearer hears.

== 2. Evidence base ==

Lehrer & Gevirtz 2014 review the resonance-frequency-biofeedback
literature: effect sizes <math>d \approx 0.4\!-\!0.8</math> on
stress / anxiety / PTSD / depression endpoints across dozens of
trials. Goessl et al. 2017 meta-analysis confirms
<math>d \approx 0.3\!-\!0.6</math> on similar endpoints.
HeartMath Institute (McCraty et al. 2009) replicates on
healthy-adult cognitive-performance endpoints.

This is the '''empirical floor''' of the
[[Heart_Rate_Variability_and_Psi|HRV-and-ψ]] claim: the
state demonstrably benefits wearers regardless of whether it also
enables ψ-coupling.

== 3. Operator-state framing ==

The framework's testable extension is that operators in coherent
state show stronger effects on blinded ψ-tasks. This is
unproven and is the Mk2.0 / Mk3.0 framework-contribution endpoint.

